  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "Daybook",
      "text": "Daybook is a local-first intelligent journal writing assistant for DeepSeek Harness. You choose one ordinary folder; Daybook uses the model and your Writing Instruction to lightly polish explicit journal requests, writes only the new entry, organizes today's entry behind a confirmation preview, copies current-message images, and keeps guarded undo receipts in Harness's private data directory."
    },
    {
      "section": "Daybook",
      "text": "> Alpha status: the source is public for testing. The package is not published to npm or the DSH Plugin marketplace."
    },
    {
      "section": "What it writes",
      "text": "```text\nyour-journal/\n├── 2026-08-14.md\n└── images/\n    └── 2026-08-14/\n\nThere is no hidden Daybook folder in your journal. Preview and undo data live under `$DSH_HOME/daybook/v1/`.\n\nDaybook uses standard Markdown image links and does not require Obsidian. If the selected folder is inside an Obsidian vault, Obsidian simply becomes one optional viewer."
    },
    {
      "section": "Alpha behavior",
      "text": "- **Smart capture:** ask “记到日志里” (or another explicit journal request), or enter `/daybook <text>`. The normal model turn applies the active Writing Instruction to clean up punctuation, obvious grammar, speech fillers, and accidental repetition in only the new material, calls `daybook_capture`, and reports the actual saved text and receipt. Earlier text is never rewritten.\n- **Raw fallback:** `/daybook-raw <text>` is an explicit no-model, text-only path that trims and saves the text as-is. It never saves images and is not the normal Daybook workflow.\n- **Organize today:** ask to organize today's journal. Daybook reads the complete daily file, presents a read-only rewrite preview, and asks for explicit confirmation in the conversation. Only after the user confirms does the model call `daybook_apply_organize`.\n- **Images:** images can only come from the latest direct user message. They are copied, never moved. Privacy protection is on by default.\n- **Undo:** each applied mutation has an id. Undo succeeds only if the file still matches that operation; otherwise it safely refuses.\n- **External edits:** a preview never overwrites a file changed after the preview was created."
    },
    {
      "section": "Alpha behavior",
      "text": "The browser slash menu offers `daybook` as a plain-text insertion source. It deliberately does not claim submit, so `/daybook <text>` remains a normal model prompt. Only the explicit `/daybook-raw` fallback is registered as a direct content-bearing command; use a Harness build that supports `CommandDefinition.engagesSession` so its durable input and receipt retain the current Session and New Session creates a different conversation."
    },
    {
      "section": "Local development install",
      "text": "Requirements: Node.js 22.19+ and pnpm.\n\nThe current Alpha is tested against DeepSeek Harness `0.1.0-rc.6`; its DSH peer packages are supplied by the active Harness profile.\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/franksong2702/dsh-daybook.git\ncd dsh-daybook\npnpm install --config.minimum-release-age=0\npnpm run check\n\nThen open **Settings → Plugins → Plugin configuration → Daybook**, choose a journal folder, and save."
    },
    {
      "section": "Local development install",
      "text": "The bundle patch only adds Daybook with an empty folder setting. It does not change the active model, web-search provider, workspace, or any existing journal."
    },
    {
      "section": "Security boundary",
      "text": "- The selected folder is the plugin's explicit standing filesystem capability.\n- Daily filenames and image subpaths are generated by Daybook, not accepted from the model.\n- Symlinked daily files and image directories are rejected.\n- Mutations are atomic and serialized across plugin processes.\n- Card actions accept only local same-origin Web requests.\n- Remote image URLs are never fetched.\n- Custom instructions and private receipts stay local and contain no account credentials."
    },
    {
      "section": "Security boundary",
      "text": "See [the Alpha contract](docs/ALPHA-SPEC.md) and [design notes](docs/DESIGN.md)."
    },
    {
